Habitat density layer; satellite by default

A derived surface, not a survey: how much duck water sits within about two
miles of each point, marsh weighted heaviest, then ponds and lakes, then
rivers. Built on a 500 m grid in Web Mercator so it maps to a screen
rectangle exactly, blurred to a 2.5 km neighbourhood, and normalised on the
99.5th percentile inside each state so a few big lakes do not flatten it.

It is labelled habitat, not birds, in the layer name and the key. There is
no open dataset of waterfowl density at a resolution that would mean
anything here - eBird Status and Trends is the right data and is not open,
and the USGS layers are 50 km cells. Drawing a bird-density gradient from
nothing would look authoritative over exactly the marsh where someone makes
a decision.

Massachusetts marsh comes from NWI emergent and estuarine wetlands over an
acre, since the state hydrography has no marsh class. The Atlantic itself
arrives as a single 2.6 million acre polygon and is excluded.

Satellite with street and place names is now the default view.

